	<title>The Other Woman</title>
	<isbn>0452287146</isbn>
	<publication_year>2006</publication_year>
	<language_code>eng</language_code>
	<description>Newly engaged, Ellie is thrilled to be accepted into the loving Cooper clan—which seems like the perfect family she never had—until she begins to realize that Dan’s mom, Linda, is a little too involved. Dan and Linda talk on the phone every day. Twice a day. As Dan and Ellie’s intimate civil wedding ceremony gets transformed into a black-tie affair, Ellie begins to wonder if it’s possible to marry the man without marrying his mother. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;As troubles mount, Ellie turns to her friends—glamorous Lisa, who always looks like she’s just stepped off a runway, and wonderfully frazzled Trish—and tries to rediscover the independence she once had, and the man she still loves. But it seems that having a child and saving a marriage means growing up in ways she’d never imagined . . .&lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;i&gt;A warm, witty, and wise look at mothers-in-law and what they teach us about ourselves, &lt;b&gt;The Other Woman&lt;/b&gt; is sure to please &lt;b&gt;Jane Green’s&lt;/b&gt; growing legion of fans.&lt;/i&gt;</description>
